  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 254 AM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nUpper-level flow on Saturday will remain fairly strong out of the \nsouthwest as the low responsible for the rainier weather marches \neastward across the Four Corners region after elongating southward \non Friday. This low will slowly turn northeastward Saturday night \ninto early Sunday morning, looking to impact the CWA a second time \non Sunday. 500mb CVA will increase across the upper-level low, \nincreasing its advection northeastward, while also ejecting multiple \nlobes of vorticity into the CWA. 700mb flow will be weaker as the \n700mb low over southern Canada move far enough away to not longer \ninfluence the region. Mostly light and variable flow is expected \nthroughout the day Saturday. Saturday night into early Sunday \nmorning, 700mb flow turns southeasterly with surface flow flipping \naround to the east. The surface low that was progged to develop in \nyesterday's model suite no longer exists, with northerly surface \nflow prevailing across eastern Colorado due to the strong cold \nfront. This will not stop precipitation from developing with \nmultiple 500mb vorticity maxima moving across the region. The NAEFS \nmodel suggests increased precipitable water values around the 90th \npercentile for southeastern portions of the CWA, where the best \nforcing looks to set up. A rain/snow mix could develop west of the \nLaramie Range with mostly rain and, maybe, a few flurries east of \nthe Laramie Range. The I-80 Summit may see a prolonged period of \nrain and snow due to easterly, upslope flow ongoing through the \nafternoon hours. 700mb temperatures cool into the 0 to 2C range \nbehind the front, leading to a much cooler day across the region \nwith surface temperatures in the mid-40s to mid-50s. Overnight lows \nSaturday night into Sunday morning will drop into the 30s \neverywhere, with 700mb temperatures between -2 and 0C. With \nprecipitation likely continuing into the overnight hours, snow \nflurries will be possible for Sunday morning, though significant \naccumulation is not expected.\n\nOn Sunday, the upper-level low is progged to get absorbed into the \nupper-level flow and finally push the upper-level trough through the \nIntermountain West by Monday morning. Residual upper-level forcing \nwill remain across the area as the low moves off to the east, \ntherefore, keeping precipitation chances possible on Sunday. 700mb \ntemperatures will remain in the -2 to 0C range throughout the day \nSunday. Easterly flow will continue Sunday due to a secondary, \nbackdoor cold front moving through the region, favoring upslope \ndevelopment along and east of the Laramie Range. While 700mb \ntemperatures will be favorable for snow, rather than rain, daytime \nwarming may prevent snow from falling with mostly a cold rain and \nflurries possible east of the Laramie Range. Once again, significant \nsnowfall accumulation is not anticipated at this time, though things \nmay change as this system moves into the shorter-term forecast \nperiods.\n\nMonday onwards favors an active period of weather with multiple \nAlbert Clipper systems pushing out of Canada and into the CONUS. \nTemperatures will remain cooler throughout the weak, near normal for \nthis time of year. Temperatures look to start warming Tuesday \nthrough the end of the week, with above average temperatures \npossible Tuesday through the end of the week. Multiple rounds of \nprecipitation will be possible throughout the week, though \nconfidence is lower in exactly how many places will see the \nprecipitation.",
